<Base
  lang="en"
  page="overview"
  eyebrow="A home system, built for a hackathon"
  title="The home system"
  description="Lanternina runs parent-approved activities on paper and e-paper. A handheld camera returns completed sheets. Language, vision and image inference runs in Microsoft Foundry."
>
  <p class="lede">Lanternina divides execution between a home hub and a cloud API. The hub coordinates displays, printing and returned sheets, and manages activity timing. The cloud API calls models on Microsoft Foundry to generate activities and pages, interpret returned sheets and prepare continuations.</p>

  <p>The hub can sit near the router. The displays communicate with it over Wi-Fi; the printer and scanner are also reached over the network.</p>

  <p>The displays run on batteries and can be placed where they are useful; more can be added. Displays and portable devices are designed for very low power consumption, aiming for weeks of availability between charges. They spend much of their time asleep and wake to communicate; e-paper retains its image while asleep. Battery life depends on update frequency and use. The hub and printer use a fixed power supply.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/camera-prototype-game.webp"><img src="/images/camera-prototype-game.webp" width="1600" height="1200" alt="The camera and a dragon model beside a display inviting the player to turn three objects in the room into places on a map with a secret passage." /></a>
  </figure>

  <h2>How an activity goes</h2>

  <p>The game develops through several exchanges until it closes. Lanternina speaks through printed pages and display messages. The adolescent replies by writing or drawing on the sheets and photographing what they made. The returned material shapes the next response, which can bring another sheet.</p>
  <p>The pace is a material correspondence. A reply takes time while the photograph is read, a page is prepared and a display refreshes. Instructions remain on paper during the wait. Prompts request a default ceiling of 5 sheets for the whole game, distributed across its exchanges and including any printed closing response. The parent can choose a lower ceiling; the game need not use every sheet.</p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <b>The parent steers the proposal.</b> They write interests, subjects to avoid, language and current circumstances. They can prepare a brief and set the available hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The cloud API requests an activity from a model.</b> It sends household preferences and a selected activity method. The model returns a graph of steps, called moments, with duration options, help and paths to the ending.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The API checks and stores the activity.</b> It validates the response format and activity structure, and applies Content Safety to the text. Failed checks can trigger a repair attempt. The parent reviews the accepted activity and can approve or refuse it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The hub collects the activity.</b> It polls the device API on its own schedule. The API returns the waiting activity in that HTTPS response, and the hub stores it locally before preparing the first page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The hub requests a page from the cloud API and prints it.</b> The image model receives the content and drawing instructions for that page. The API returns the generated image; the hub sends it to the printer over IPP.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The camera or scanner returns the completed page.</b> A vision model receives the photograph or scan and the original page image. It compares them and returns a reading of the marks that were added.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The hub selects the next moment from the reading.</b> It follows the corresponding transition in the activity graph and prepares the next message or page. Further replies on paper and photographs continue the game until its ending. At its first timer check within thirty minutes of the end time set by the parent, it starts the current moment's path to the ending.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="camera">Returning the sheet with the camera</h2>
  <p>The adolescent photographs the completed sheet to return it to the activity. The camera uses a XIAO ESP32S3 Sense in a 3D-printed enclosure, with a shutter button, a feedback light and a battery. The prototype produces JPEGs at 1600 × 1200 pixels.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/camera-prototype-2.webp"><img src="/images/camera-prototype-2.webp" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" alt="The assembled white camera, with its lens on the front and a green shutter button and yellow feedback light on top." /></a>
    <figcaption>The assembled prototype, with its shutter button and feedback light.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>A press starts acquisition. The light stays on until the selected frame is ready; saving and delivery continue after it goes out. The firmware writes the JPEG to local flash and reads it back before starting Wi-Fi. It sends the image to the home hub over authenticated HTTPS. Two flashes confirm a matching receipt after the hub has stored it. A failed delivery keeps the photograph and its identifier for a later attempt.</p>
  <p>For a printed sheet, the reader receives the photograph and the original page kept for printing. It compares them despite changes in perspective and lighting, transcribes legible additions and reports uncertainty. An uncertain reading or ambiguous activity association leaves the activity waiting. The hub chooses one compatible camera or scanner from the parent's assignments and keeps that choice for the return. The family can view, download and delete photographs in the parent's panel.</p>
  <p>A construction or arrangement can instead be photographed without a printed original. The model describes relevant visible material. An invited adolescent can also send photographs through <a href="https://app.lanternina.com/portal">the portal</a>; the cloud stores them until the hub retrieves them. Only a photograph associated with a compatible waiting moment enters the activity's reading path.</p>

  <figure class="narrow">
    <a href="/images/camera-prototype-1.webp"><img src="/images/camera-prototype-1.webp" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The open camera enclosure showing the controller board, antenna, wiring, shutter and battery beside it." /></a>
    <figcaption>The open enclosure shows the board, wiring and battery. The photograph documents the prototype, not a wiring diagram.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Prototype trials verified battery-powered capture, light feedback, sleep, wake and resumed delivery after a receiver interruption. <a href="/en/status/#camera-checks">The implementation page describes the checks and remaining limits.</a></p>

  <h2>The parent's panel</h2>

  <p>The parent opens <a href="https://app.lanternina.com">the panel</a> in a browser. Waiting activities show their title, duration, planned sheets and a description of what will happen. The parent can read the script, approve, refuse or work on the idea. Approval makes the activity available to the house without starting it immediately.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/panel-activities-valle-lunga-en.webp"><img src="/images/panel-activities-valle-lunga-en.webp" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="The panel showing the Valle Lunga railway activity, its expandable script and the Approve, Refuse and Work on it controls." /></a>
    <figcaption>Activities awaiting approval, with invented data. The sample activity is in Italian.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Settings cover days and hours, how many activities may begin, interests, subjects to avoid and language. The parent can add a temporary note and set bounds on how a continuation may change. During an activity, they can bring its end time forward or ask it to start closing. The activity log collects what the system generated and showed.</p>

  <h3>Devices in the house</h3>
  <p>The panel lists registered displays and cameras, and discovered printers and scanners. The parent names devices, assigns jobs and sees which need attention. Cameras and scanners offer one return function, "shows Lanternina". Several compatible devices can hold it; the hub selects one for each return. Displays can hold several display jobs.</p>
  <p>The parent sets each display's connection interval. If all intervals exceed 5 minutes, the panel warns that at least one display assigned to activity instructions should connect every 5 minutes or less, so replies do not take too long. A shorter interval reduces the wait for an update and requires more battery-powered wakeups. The interval does not guarantee the total response time, which also includes processing and printing.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/panel-devices-en.webp"><img src="/images/panel-devices-en.webp" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="The panel's Devices page with editable names, display status and checkboxes for assigning jobs." /></a>
    <figcaption>Device names and jobs, with invented data.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Reconstructing a railway</h2>

  <p>
    An old railway connected a hut to a cutting, passing between rows of trees and over a bridge. The map preserves these observations but leaves the route beside the pond uncertain. The activity asks for a possible route in the notebook, its direction and an alternative.
  </p>

