  <p>In this synthetic example, the reader recognized the written arrow and direction. The continuation asked for them to be revised after a new indication of the map's orientation.</p>

  <p>
    The system distinguishes a filled sheet from a blank one and uses what it reads to continue. If it cannot read the sheet, it waits. The continuation passes safety checks without a second parent approval.
  </p>

  <h2 id="memory">Memory</h2>
  <p>The system stores configuration, generated activities, endings, reported durations and reading-derived summaries. A separate call observes scanner page comparisons to calibrate load and writing; photographic reads skip that call. Recent observations help compute the profile that steers prompts. The panel does not expose that profile as a score. The parent can clear activity memory and observations. A complete plain-language memory view is not built.</p>
  <p>During development, photographs, returned material, readings and diagnostics may be retained for the authenticated family. The photo archive, generated pages, activity trail and profile observations have separate storage and deletion paths. Original scanner pixels can be archived locally when the scan database is configured; a complete scan-gallery workflow is not built.</p>
  <p>An administrator can additionally enable a temporary archive of complete readings for 14 days. Its entries expire 14 days after writing. This is not a 14-day deletion rule for all photographs, traces, summaries or observations. Deleting a photograph does not automatically delete every interpretation derived from it. Retention policy will be reviewed after development.</p>

  <h2>The stack</h2>

  <h3>In the house</h3>
  <p>
    The prototype uses a Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5 on a PoE base board with Raspberry Pi OS, 7.5" displays on XIAO ESP32-S3 with patched TRMNL firmware, and an Epson ET-2870 over IPP and eSCL. In the panel, the parent names devices and assigns display jobs. One display can follow activities, show reminders and hold pictures, or have only some of these jobs.
  </p>

