  <h3>In the cloud</h3>
  <p>Static Web Apps serves the site and panel. The API in Container Apps receives requests from the panel and hub, calls models on Microsoft Foundry and applies Content Safety to generated content. Cosmos DB stores configuration and records; Blob Storage stores images. The scan-reading path does not apply inbound-image Content Safety.</p>

  <CloudComponents lang="en" />

  <h2>Boundaries enforced in code</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>The activity itself provides the reason to continue; the system does not add streaks, daily targets or withheld rewards.</li>
    <li>The scheduler responds to approved activities and their deadlines, rather than absence or time-spent metrics.</li>
    <li>
      The review gate makes the profile available only to the models that write and run an activity.
      User-facing displays, printed sheets and panel pages receive reviewed activity content.
    </li>
    <li>The vision path compares returned sheets or describes photographed objects. It requests visible material and uncertainty, not identity, emotion or attention assessment. Photographs can nevertheless contain people or private information; a prompt does not guarantee that a model will omit every unsupported inference.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Why each boundary exists, and what it costs</a
    > · <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/docs/prompts`}>every prompt, whole, as the model receives it</a>
  </p>
  <h2>Between activities</h2>

  <p>The displays stay in the rooms between activities. During that time they serve as small framed pictures: instead of an empty screen or an instruction left over from a finished activity, they show an image that belongs on a wall or a shelf. When needed, the same display can show a reminder or accompany a new activity, then return to its picture.</p>

  <h3>Pictures</h3>
  <p>The parent assigns the picture job to displays and chooses image themes, hours and how often pictures change. Generated pictures remain available to browse in the panel. A dedicated display can keep showing its picture while another follows an activity.</p>

